WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Native C++ pipelines benefit from layered checks.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:18.120
Compiler warnings, warning policies, static analysis, formatting or linting where useful, unit tests, integration tests, dependency scanning, SBOM generation checks, and packaging validation can all catch problems before release.

3
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.460
The goal is not to prove perfection.

4
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.240
The goal is to provide timely feedback on classes of defects that are common in native systems.

5
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.300
Each check should have a clear reason for existing.

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.660
Sanitizers can be valuable when used in suitable build modes.

7
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:46.400
AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, ThreadSanitizer where useful, and related runtime checks can reveal memory, lifetime, concurrency, and undefined-behavior issues during tests.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:55.460
They may not run in every release configuration, but scheduled jobs, nightly builds, or targeted test suites can still provide strong signal.

9
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.240
Teams should document which platforms and test suites use them.

10
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.000
Security checks need workflow design.

11
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.020
Fast pull-request checks should give developers quick feedback.

12
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:16.280
Slower analysis, broader platform coverage, fuzzing at a defensive level for parsers and boundary code, and deeper dependency review may fit scheduled or release jobs.

13
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.420
This keeps the pipeline useful without turning every small change into an unreasonably long wait.

14
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.000
Clear scheduling also helps teams know which checks are required before release.

15
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.760
Findings need owners, triage rules, and documented exceptions.

16
00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:39.660
If warnings are ignored, scanners produce unowned tickets, or sanitizer failures become routine noise, the value of the pipeline drops quickly.

17
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:53.440
A mature CI/CD process treats security findings as engineering work: assign them, prioritize them, record exceptions, and revisit exceptions when the code, tool, or release risk changes.

18
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.100
The pipeline should make risk visible without drowning teams in unreviewed alerts.

19
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.680
Quality improves when exceptions expire or require periodic review by the responsible team before release.
